 Cerro Las Palomas is characterized by dense thickets of small trees and shrubs from common genera, typically with twisted stems and multiple basal branches. Epiphytic plants are frequently observed growing up to 3 meters above the ground, on mosscovered trunks and branches. The most abundant species in the area include Clusia thurifera Planch. & Triana, Clusia callejasii Pipoly, Guzmania squarrosa (Mez & Sodiro) L. B. Sm. & Pittendr. and Weinmannia elliptica Kunth (Alzate et al. 2016). The new species grows epiphytically, typically on the basal parts of live or dead tree trunks, and occasionally on vertical substrates such as the middle sections of tree trunks (Figure 5). Flowering appears to peak in June and July, with additional records from September and January. However, no flowering individuals were observed during a field visit on January 8 th, 2020.","","text","",""]]},"VBW3C":{"d":[["","CO","iso","",""]]},"VC9LG":{"d":[["NP","NP","iso","",""]]},"VHT3":{"d":[["S-Tibet (Nyalam)","","text","",""]]},"W74C":{"d":[["Oriental(Sri Lanka)","","text","",""]]},"X7PF":{"d":[["","50","tdwg","native",""],["Australia","","text","native",""]]},"XFR5":{"d":[["","ZAI","tdwg","",""]]},"Z25D":{"d":[["Afghanistan (Herat)","","text","",""]]},"Z5NH":{"d":[["RUSOO","","text","native",""]]},"ZDP7":{"d":[["La Réunion","","text","",""]]},"ZP8C":{"d":[["","ZAM","tdwg","",""]]}}